tml":"<p>Original supplied handwritten marking key.</p><figure><img src=\"assets/chemistry/practice-import-additional/9ecfc0fa42be75a6f5e6a93b.webp\" alt=\"Supplied mole marking key question 2\" data-word-width-px=\"640\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" data-word-max-height=\"7\"></figure>","options":[],"optionsHtml":[],"correctAnswer":null,"markingMode":"manual","solutionOrigin":"supplied","sourceFile":"Test 3 2015.docx","sourcePath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/11/John XXIII/2015/Test 3 2015.docx","sourceBlobSHA":"6fc9cf32b79b89aa158ee3de6836b701d245facf","sourceSHA256":"595cd312fc0e689b2f8f3e38ebd0e04cd56d92d8bceb4df8d841ff0d208b860b","sourceKeyFile":"test 3 Mole answers.pdf","sourceKeyPath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/11/John XXIII/2015/test 3 Mole answers.pdf","sourceKeyBlobSHA":"fb249f7f50d71dc60078469b4e96b071c2363a78","sourceKeySHA256":"7336ad1f89e75933f4a646d45c0eb751cbb3b0edb2202b48d4983aca7f576910","sourceKeyNumber":2,"bankFile":"practice-studio-chemistry-john-mole2015.json","addedAt":"2026-11-07T00:21:00.500397Z","editableText":true,"questionFormat":"native-text","fontFamily":"Arial","fontSizePt":12,"responseLines":0,"sourceReviewNotes":[],"sourceTextFile":"Test 3 2015.docx","sourceQuestionOccurrence":1,"types":["Short answer"],"figures":0,"hash":"651aac16ce4422075edfeff14f6f1e17ff834f70b51c7a0962eee051aaf26c85"},{"id":"chemistry-additional-john-mole2015-written-3","subject":"Chemistry","year":11,"examYear":2015,"units":"1 & 2","number":3,"source":"Moles and Chemical Reactions (John XXIII, 2015)","section":"Short answer","marks":5,"topics":["Chemical calculations","Solutions and acids"],"subtopics":["Moles mass and composition","Stoichiometry limiting reagents and yield","Acids bases and indicators"],"mixed":true,"text":"5 marks A 15.3 g piece of steel, containing only iron and carbon, was treated with an excess of hot hydrochloric acid to form 3.02 L of hydrogen at STP. Fe + 2 HCl → FeCl2 + H2 a. Work out the number of moles of Hydrogen gas (H2) produced. (1) b. Work out the number of moles of iron used up in this reaction. (1) c. Work out the mass of iron used up. (1) d. Calculate the percentage of iron in the steel. Use appropriate number of significant figures. (2)","html":"<div><p><strong>5 marks</strong></p>\n<p>A 15.3 g piece of steel, containing only iron and carbon, was treated\nwith an excess of hot hydrochloric acid to form 3.02 L of hydrogen at\nSTP.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Fe + 2 HCl → FeCl<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>a. Work out the number of moles of Hydrogen gas (H<sub>2</sub>)\nproduced. (1)</p>\n<p>b. Work out the number of moles of iron used up in this reaction.\n(1)</p>\n<p>c. Work out the mass of iron used up. (1)</p>\n<p>d. Calculate the percentage of iron in the steel.</p>\n<p><strong>Use appropriate number of significant figures.</strong>\n(2)</p>\n</div>","answerHtml":"<p>Original supplied handwritten marking key.</p><figure><img src=\"assets/chemistry/practice-import-additional/6fc008413a369da6bea1af2d.webp\" alt=\"Supplied mole marking key question 3\" data-word-width-px=\"640\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" data-word-max-height=\"7\"></figure>","options":[],"optionsHtml":[],"correctAnswer":null,"markingMode":"manual","solutionOrigin":"supplied","sourceFile":"Test 3 2015.docx","sourcePath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/11/John XXIII/2015/Test 3 2015.docx","sourceBlobSHA":"6fc9cf32b79b89aa158ee3de6836b701d245facf","sourceSHA256":"595cd312fc0e689b2f8f3e38ebd0e04cd56d92d8bceb4df8d841ff0d208b860b","sourceKeyFile":"test 3 Mole answers.pdf","sourceKeyPath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/11/John XXIII/2015/test 3 Mole answers.pdf","sourceKeyBlobSHA":"fb249f7f50d71dc60078469b4e96b071c2363a78","sourceKeySHA256":"7336ad1f89e75933f4a646d45c0eb751cbb3b0edb2202b48d4983aca7f576910","sourceKeyNumber":3,"bankFile":"practice-studio-chemistry-john-mole2015.json","addedAt":"2026-11-07T00:21:00.500397Z","editableText":true,"questionFormat":"native-text","fontFamily":"Arial","fontSizePt":12,"responseLines":0,"sourceReviewNotes":[],"sourceTextFile":"Test 3 2015.docx","sourceQuestionOccurrence":1,"types":["Short answer"],"figures":0,"hash":"2686452859356e81d1579f0b068b8f2248aa3d5675584a1b4a1c7d8803bd479b"},{"id":"chemistry-additional-john-mole2015-written-4","subject":"Chemistry","year":11,"examYear":2015,"units":"1 & 2","number":4,"source":"Moles and Chemical Reactions (John XXIII, 2015)","section":"Short answer","marks":3,"topics":["Chemical reactions"],"subtopics":["Formulae nomenclature and equations"],"mixed":false,"text":"Balance the following chemical equations: (3 marks) (a) __ Pb(CO3)2 (s) + □ HNO3 (aq) → □ Pb(NO3)4 (aq + □ H2O (l) + □ CO2 (g) (b) □ Cr (s) + □ HCl(aq) → □ CrCl3 (aq) + □ H2 (g) (c) □ NH4OH (aq) + □ H3PO4 (aq) → □ (NH4)3PO4 (aq) + □ H2O (l)","html":"<div><p><strong>Balance the following chemical equations: (3\nmarks)</strong></p>\n<p>(a) __ Pb(CO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2 (s)</sub> + □ HNO<sub>3 (aq)</sub> →\n □ Pb(NO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>4 (aq</sub> + □ H<sub>2</sub>O\n<sub>(l)</sub> + □ CO<sub>2 (g)</sub></p>\n<p>(b) □ Cr <sub>(s</sub>) + □ HCl(aq) → □ CrCl<sub>3 (aq)</sub> +\n □ H<sub>2 (g)</sub></p>\n<p>(c) □ NH<sub>4</sub>OH <sub>(aq)</sub> + □ H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>4\n(aq)</sub> → □ (NH<sub>4</sub>)<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>4 (aq)</sub> +\n □ H<sub>2</sub>O <sub>(l)</sub></p>\n</div>","answerHtml":"<p>Original supplied handwritten marking key.</p><figure><img src=\"assets/chemistry/practice-import-additional/e2c74d4fc2b29cb833adefca.webp\" alt=\"Supplied mole marking key question 4\" data-word-width-px=\"640\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" data-word-max-height=\"7\"></figure>","options":[],"optionsHtml":[],"correctAnswer":null,"markingMode":"manual","solutionOrigin":"supplied","sourceFile":"Test 3 2015.docx","sourcePath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/11/John XXIII/2015/Test 3 2015.docx","sourceBlobSHA":"6fc9cf32b79b89aa158ee3de6836b701d245facf","sourceSHA256":"595cd312fc0e689b2f8f3